<div class="code">
<pre>
<code>if (dollar.value = null || dollar.wrinkled)
{
return dollar;
}</code>
</pre></div>
<p>This is the &quot;Crux&quot; of the issue. So usually when you do a comparison, you do</p>
<div class="code">
<pre>
<code>variable == testValue</code>
</pre></div>
<p>By doing a single = sign, instead of doing a comparison, you're assigning the right hand side value to the variable. Since it's null, the &quot;Dollar&quot; object (I assume it's OO based in this example as this is C-derivative code, most likely C#, Java, or C++) no longer has any data. Thus &quot;erasing&quot; the concept of a dollar.</p>

							</div>

								
					
