			<div class="content" id="post-content-3833340">
									

<p>A yoda condition reverses the normal order of a conditional statement. Mainly I think it is here because that's a kind of stupid technical argument people have instead of actually helping.</p>
<p>Apparently these are coders running the Foundation's universe as a simulation.</p>
<p>One of them, billybobjoe, discovered that some people inside the simulation found a set of statements (that fit on a notecard) that broke physics. So he deliberately introduced a bug where a vending machine in New York deleted the conceptual value of whatever you put in the dollar slot by leaving off an &quot;=&quot;. The idea was that Foundation would contain it, and then later use it to neutralize the physics breaking notecard. Which seems like an elegant solution. Then phoebtheweeb found this bug and fixed it because they weren't paying any attention to what the other guy was talking about. So the Foundation couldn't neutralize the notecard and physics broke.</p>

